  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure will continue to influence the area as it slowly \ndrifts towards the Mid-Atlantic Coast through Tuesday. With the area \nnow on the backside of the aforementioned high pressure, overall \nflow will become more southerly allowing for an increase in both \nmoisture and warmer air across the area. Highs today and tomorrow \nwill climb into the upper 60s to low 70s before really warming into \nthe mid 70s on Tuesday. As the area transitions to this southwest \nflow, conditions should remain dry today. However, late tonight \nthrough Monday there is a mid-level push of energy that is expected \nto move northeast through the area which could provide enough \nsupport for some isolated shower development across western \ncounties. At this point not expecting anything widespread or any \nheavy rain showers, however wanted to make note of the change given \nprevious forecasts were dry through Tuesday. By Monday night, the \naxis of mid-level energy should push east, allowing for conditions \nto dry out through Tuesday. No matter what, given the proximity of \nthe area to the departing high and approaching low pressure, mostly \ncloudy skies are expected to stick around. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe end of this prolonged period of dry weather looks to be in site \nas confidence continues to increase that widespread precipitation \nwill impact the area Wednesday and Thursday. Behind the departing \nhigh pressure discussed prior, a deepening upper level trough and \nassociated low pressure system over the north-central US is expected \nto track east towards the Great Lakes region. Models are slowly \nbeginning to agree on the overall timing of the system through \nThursday, which essentially keeps the surface low over the region \nand moves multiple boundaries east. This couples with strong upper \nlevel support from the trough and an open stream of moisture from \nthe Gulf should result in multiple rounds of showers in the middle \nof the week. The biggest uncertainty now is the progression of the \nsystem once it reaches the region. Models have now begun to slow the \nprogression of this low, nearly stalling it over the region as the \noverall pattern becomes less amplified. If this does occur, the \npotential for showers could extend past Thursday possibly even \nlingering into the weekend. \n\nAt this point, there are no flooding concerns with this \nprecipitation as it should be slow and steady limiting the localized \nrunoff from the dry antecedent conditions. In addition, given the \nincreasing temperatures into the mid 70s, cannot rule out a few \nrumbles of thunder on Wednesday and Thursday, although overall \ninstability looks to be marginal given the expected cloud cover and \ndecreasing daylight hours. Temperatures through next weekend are \nexpected to remain above normal.",
